// `:1` without a type is UNSIGNED. A one-bit signed integer holds only 0 and
// -1, so `Flag = 1` would store -1 and every `Flag == 1` in the program would
// be false -- a flag that is set and reads as unset. This is the check that the
// default stays width-dependent.
//
// Two bits and up keep the SIGNED default: {-2,-1,0,1} is a real range, and
// changing that would quietly alter arithmetic in programs that already exist.
#variable Flag:1 = 1              // unsigned by default -> 1
#variable Odd:1 integer = 1       // asked for signed    -> -1
#variable Two:2 = 3               // still signed        -> -1

// A #param and a #constant follow the same rule; they are read through a
// variable because the state dump only carries assignable leaves.
#param    Par:1 = 1
#constant Con:1 = 1
#variable FromPar:8 integer = 0
#variable FromCon:8 integer = 0
FromPar = Par
FromCon = Con

// The initialiser must SURVIVE the type choice. Deciding the type after the
// pattern matched left every typeless declaration zero-initialised, because the
// pattern reads the type to know what kind of constant it may accept -- so a
// width-dependent default has to be settled before the match, not after.
#variable Kept:1 = 1
#variable KeptWide:32 = -7
